(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-cam-nutrition-exam2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which principle does the lecture give for biofeedback?","choices":["\"Anything that can be measured can be changed\"","\"Let food be thy medicine\"","\"Like cures like\" in matched symptom patterns","\"Health is balance between opposing forces\""],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this as the biofeedback principle, supported by electronic monitoring that lets patients gain control over physiologic responses through training and practice. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"About how many acupuncture points had been described by 300 AD?","choices":["About 600 points","About 100 points","About 200 points","About 350 points"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says roughly 350 points were described by 300 AD. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does Traditional Chinese Medicine use the word organ, according to the lecture?","choices":["As the element to which that organ belongs","Strictly as the anatomical structure of that organ","As a synonym for the meridian that shares its name","As all the functions ascribed to that organ by Traditional Chinese Medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The concept extends beyond the anatomical structure to its ascribed functions. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which dietary supplements does the lecture identify as the most widely used of all?","choices":["Probiotic and enzyme preparations","Omega-3 fatty acid preparations","Multivitamins and minerals","Adaptogenic herbs such as ashwagandha"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states multivitamins and minerals are the most widely used dietary supplements, and that patients should take only one per day. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which of the following is a Fu organ in the lecture's classification?","choices":["Kidney","Gallbladder","Spleen","Liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Fu organs are hollow and yang: large intestine, small intestine, stomach, bladder, gallbladder, and Sanjiao. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which complication does the lecture link specifically to manipulation of the upper cervical region?","choices":["Cauda equina syndrome","Pneumothorax","Vertebral compression fracture","Cerebrovascular accident"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The estimated frequency is 1 in 5.85 million manipulations, from a study of arterial dissections. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which five elements form the basis of many traditional Chinese medicine concepts, per the lecture?","choices":["Space, air, fire, water, and earth","Blood, phlegm, bile, lymph, and marrow","Wind, heat, cold, damp, and dryness","Earth, fire, water, wood, and metal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists earth, fire, water, wood, and metal as the five elements believed to represent patterns and relationships within nature and the human body. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does petrissage involve?","choices":["Deep circular or crosswise movement with the thumbs or elbows","Smooth gliding strokes performed with oil","Lifting and kneading of skin, subcutaneous tissue and muscle","Beating or tapping with the side of the hand or the fingertips"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It frees adhesions by stretching and separating muscle fibre, fascia and scar tissue, and is often performed without lotion. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which patients should avoid glucosamine, and why?","choices":["Patients with sulfa allergy, because glucosamine is a sulfur donor","Patients with shellfish allergy, since glucosamine comes from shellfish","Patients with soy allergy, because glucosamine is extracted from soy protein","Patients with pineapple allergy, because glucosamine is a proteolytic enzyme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says glucosamine is derived from proteins and shellfish and to avoid it in those with shellfish allergies, reading labels carefully. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In which direction should forces typically be applied?","choices":["In whichever direction the patient finds most comfortable","Along the muscle fibres, though cross fibre is also used","Always perpendicular to the direction of the muscle fibres","Always from proximal to distal along the limb"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also directs pressure to be in line with venous flow. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which relationship does chiropractic focus on?","choices":["The relationship between diet and inflammation in the joints","The relationship between spinal structure and nervous system function","The relationship between muscle strength and joint stability","The relationship between posture and respiratory capacity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that this relationship affects health. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the law of minimum dose hold, and what process accompanies it?","choices":["The lower the dose, the greater the therapeutic effect, achieved through repeated dilution and shaking called succussion","The dose must be calculated by body weight, with adjustment through titration","The smallest clinically effective dose should first be identified and then maintained by continuous steady infusion","The dose should be doubled at each visit until symptoms resolve completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the law of minimum dose as the belief that lower doses produce greater therapeutic effect, and notes that many remedies undergo repeated dilution and shaking, a process called succussion. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the simillimum?","choices":["A close match between the patient's picture and a known drug picture","The first remedy tried in any new patient","The alcohol base in which the remedy is fixed","The most dilute preparation available of a given remedy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The homeopath takes a detailed physical, mental and emotional history to find it. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which body standardized the numbering and naming of acupuncture points?","choices":["The National Institutes of Health","The National Certification Commission for Acupuncture and Oriental Medicine","The World Health Organization","The Food and Drug Administration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives examples such as Sanyinjiao (SP6) and Fengchi (GB20). — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Beyond individual work, what group application does the lecture give for equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["Groups and corporate leadership, for team-building","Family therapy for bereavement","School physical education curricula","Competitive equestrian training"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states equine-facilitated learning may be used for groups and corporate leadership for team-building experiences. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does the lecture put the stroke risk from spinal manipulation in perspective?","choices":["It is roughly equal to the risk of general anaesthesia","It is 400 times less than the death rate from bleeding on anti-inflammatories","It is 40 times greater than the risk from physiotherapy","It is the leading cause of stroke in adults under 45"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture uses this comparison directly. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about breed restrictions for service dogs?","choices":["Only retrievers and shepherds qualify as service dogs","Any breed may serve, and entry cannot be refused on breed","Breed restrictions apply in housing but not in public accommodations","Breeds over 50 pounds require additional certification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states a service dog can be any type of dog breed and that a public establishment or landlord may not prohibit entry solely because it is a certain breed. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does the lecture define conventional healthcare?","choices":["A system whose practitioners treat symptoms and disease using drugs, radiation, or surgery","A system whose practitioners treat the whole person using diet, herbs, and manual therapy","A system whose practitioners restore balance among the body's three fundamental energies","A system whose practitioners direct healing energy through the hands toward the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines conventional healthcare as a system in which professionals such as physicians, physician assistants, nurse practitioners, pharmacists, physical therapists, and nurses treat symptoms and diseases using drugs, radiation, or surgery. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Gua sha is also known by which other names, and what is it used for?","choices":["Coining or spooning, used mainly for anxiety and insomnia in adult patients","Scraping, coining, or spooning, used commonly for neck and back musculoskeletal pain","Cupping or suctioning, used commonly for improving balance and physical function","Tapping or percussing, used mainly for nausea and procedure-related distress in adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives scraping, coining, and spooning as alternate names, describes repeated strokes with a smooth-edged instrument, and lists musculoskeletal pain of the neck and back as common uses. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What distinguishes a companion animal from an assistive animal?","choices":["A companion animal must be certified by a licensed professional","A companion animal has no special training for a disability","A companion animal is limited to dogs and miniature horses","A companion animal is trained to perform specific tasks on command"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines a companion animal, also called a pet, as having no special training in helping an individual cope with a disability, used for company, amusement, psychological support and extrovert display. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which explanations does the lecture offer for why hot showers relieve cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome?","choices":["Disrupted thermoregulation, heat countering CB1, or cutaneous steal","Direct antiemetic action of heat on the vomiting centre","Increased hepatic blood flow that speeds cannabis clearance","Reduced absorption of cannabinoids from subcutaneous fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture offers all three of these explanations, including CB1 receptors in the splanchnic circulation slowing gastric emptying. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"On what does chiropractic medicine primarily focus, according to the lecture?","choices":["Diagnosis and management of musculoskeletal conditions, particularly those involving the spine","Diagnosis and management of nutritional deficiency, particularly of vitamins and minerals","Diagnosis and management of stress-related disorders, particularly anxiety and insomnia","Diagnosis and management of heavy metal toxicity, particularly lead and mercury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes chiropractic medicine as focused on musculoskeletal conditions especially of the spine, using spinal manipulation and other manual therapy to improve mobility and reduce pain. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is moxibustion intended to do, according to the lecture?","choices":["Remove dead cells from the surface of the skin","Stimulate the flow of Qi and blood","Reduce the number of needles required","Sterilise the needle before insertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives pain, tendonitis, digestive issues, and menstrual problems as the conditions treated. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Above roughly what single dose of THC does the lecture say euphoria can be produced?","choices":["Over 4 mg","Over 350 mg","Over 60 mg","Over 10 mg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that over 10 mg of THC at one time can produce euphoria. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How far back does the lecture date the development of acupuncture?","choices":["Roughly 150 years, beginning with Chinese immigration to the United States","Roughly 4,000 years, as part of the system of Traditional Chinese Medicine","Roughly 400 years, beginning with European contact in the 1600s","Roughly 1,000 years, beginning in Korea and Japan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates it to about 4,000 years ago within Traditional Chinese Medicine. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the primary role of an emotional support animal?","choices":["Performing trained tasks that mitigate a disability","Alerting the public that the owner has a disability","Delivering goal-directed treatment alongside a therapist","Emotional comfort, companionship, friendship and affection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as the primary role of an emotional support animal. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"From which plant source is the drug digitalis (digoxin) derived?","choices":["Willow bark","Cinchona tree","Periwinkle","Foxglove"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists digitalis (digoxin) as a derivative of foxglove. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient describes a clinic offering an infusion that \"cures cancer,\" available only at a facility abroad. Based on the lecture's counseling questions, which feature is the strongest warning sign?","choices":["The practitioner makes unfounded claims such as the ability to cure cancer","The therapy is delivered by infusion rather than by mouth","The therapy has been studied in more than one clinical trial","The treatment is being offered through an established academic medical institution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists unfounded claims such as curing cancer among the specific questions clinicians should raise with patients, alongside travel requirements and cost. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which organs does the lecture classify as Zang?","choices":["The hollow yang organs: large intestine, small intestine, stomach, bladder, gallbladder","Only the organs belonging to the fire element","The organs with no meridian of their own","The solid yin organs: lung, heart, spleen, kidney, liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pericardium and the conception vessel are also listed with them. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the estimated frequency of cauda equina syndrome following manipulation?","choices":["About 1 per 1,000 manipulations","About 1 per 100,000 manipulations","About 1 per 5.85 million manipulations","About 1 per 100 million manipulations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It may follow lumbar manipulation with disc herniation, bowel and bladder disturbance, saddle anaesthesia, and leg weakness. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"For which indications did the 2015 meta-analysis cited in the lecture include randomised controlled trials?","choices":["Only chronic pain and spasticity due to multiple sclerosis","Chemotherapy nausea, appetite, pain, spasticity, mood, sleep, glaucoma","Only psychiatric indications such as depression, anxiety, and sleep disorder","Only conditions on the Florida automatic qualifying list"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all of these indications for the 2015 meta-analysis and notes most studies showed only modest symptom improvements. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"In the lecture's chain of reasoning, what does loss of normal movement lead to first?","choices":["Immediate mechanical failure of the joint","Chemical changes within the tissues","Changes in how the tissues are stressed and replenished","Compression of the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those changes then cause chemical changes, which predispose the tissues to mechanical failure. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the relationship between yin and yang?","choices":["Independent of one another, acting separately","Identical forces under two different names","Interdependent, interconsuming, and intertransforming","Permanently opposed, with one always dominant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that they should always be in balance to maintain health. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which tasks does the lecture list for a psychiatric service dog?","choices":["Pressing alarm buttons and breaking a person's fall","Guiding a handler around obstacles and reading traffic signals","Medication reminders, room searches, and interrupting self-injury","Signalling the handler when a doorbell or telephone rings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these tasks for a psychiatric service dog. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say horsemanship duties themselves can be therapeutic?","choices":["Because the physical labour substitutes for prescribed exercise","Because it demonstrates the client's authority over the animal","Feeding, watering and grooming build routine, structure and empathy","Because it reduces the cost of the therapy programme"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses must be fed, watered, exercised and groomed, and that providing care is therapeutic by establishing routines and structure while the act of caring and nurturing builds empathy. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What physiologic changes does the lecture associate with the relaxation response?","choices":["Faster breathing, increased heart rate, and higher blood pressure","Slower breathing, decreased heart rate, and lower blood pressure","Unchanged vital signs with improvement only in reported mood","Rising body temperature with a fall in measured muscle tension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that relaxation therapy promotes the relaxation response, which counteracts the physiologic effects of stress and is associated with slower breathing, decreased heart rate, and lower blood pressure. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What did the Agency for Health Care Policy and Research conclude in 1994?","choices":["That spinal manipulation should be avoided in acute back pain","That spinal manipulation was equivalent to bed rest","That spinal manipulation topped the options for certain back pain","That spinal manipulation required specialist referral in all cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among 23 options for low back pain, spinal manipulation and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs had the most beneficial effect. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture define equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["A treatment strategy that uses equine movement to reach functional goals","A certification pathway for therapy animal handlers","A mental health treatment addressing psychological disorders","An experiential approach building life skills through equine activities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of equine-facilitated learning. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which mechanism does the lecture give for capsaicin's pain-relieving effect on the skin?","choices":["It blocks sodium channels in peripheral nerve endings","It reduces substance P, which carries pain messages to the brain","It stops the release of inflammatory cytokines and interleukins","It inhibits 5-alpha-reductase in the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states capsaicin reduces substance P, which carries pain messages to the brain, and also has a thermogenic effect that increases local circulation. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which use of animals in therapy does the lecture date to 600 BC?","choices":["Companionship with dogs for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder","Florence Nightingale recommending small companion animals for the chronically ill","Integration of animals into a German community for people with disabilities","Ancient Greeks documenting horse-assisted therapy to raise the spirits of the incurably ill"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates horse-assisted therapy to raise the spirits of the incurably ill to the ancient Greeks at 600 BC. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the NADA protocol refer to in the lecture?","choices":["A scalp needling protocol used in rehabilitation after a stroke","A cosmetic facial protocol used to soften lines and scars","A needle-free technique that relies only on ear seeds and tacks","The National Acupuncture Detoxification Association ear protocol, for anxiety, depression, and substance abuse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also notes military use for stress, post-traumatic stress disorder, and pain control. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"When and by whom was homeopathy founded, according to the lecture?","choices":["In 1895, by Daniel Palmer in Iowa","In the early 1900s, in Japan","In the early 19th century, by a Swedish physician","Around 1814, by a German physician"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as having been around for more than 200 years and working side by side with conventional medicine. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise Western or medical acupuncture?","choices":["A formal rejection of acupuncture as an ineffective and unproven therapy","Less holistic, using a Western diagnostic model, and more likely used by Western-trained clinicians","An approach restricted by law to fully licensed acupuncturists only","Identical to Traditional Chinese Medicine in both its theory and its practice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture includes dry needling for musculoskeletal pain within this approach. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why should patients taking multiminerals avoid salt substitutes?","choices":["To avoid an interaction with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","To avoid reducing the effect of blood pressure medications","To prevent the supplement from binding to milk or antacids","To prevent elevated potassium levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that to prevent elevated potassium levels, patients taking multiminerals should not use salt substitutes. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does the lecture define adaptogens?","choices":["Herbs that reliably raise blood pressure and heart rate under stress","Natural substances that help the body adapt to stress and rebalance","Plant compounds that act only on the central nervous system to induce sedation","Concentrated plant extracts standardized by the FDA for potency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines adaptogens this way and notes they restore endocrine balance, modulate immunity, and support homeostasis. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which description best matches the lecture's account of functional medicine?","choices":["A whole-person approach focused on potential underlying contributors to disease, considering lifestyle, nutrition, environment, genetics, and psychosocial factors","A system of care organized around balancing three fundamental body energies determined at birth","A therapy that uses electronic monitoring so patients can learn to control physiologic responses","An approach built entirely on the principle that a substance capable of producing symptoms in a healthy person can be used to treat those same symptoms in someone who is ill"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes functional medicine as whole-person and patient-centered, focused on underlying contributors to disease and weighing lifestyle, nutrition, environment, genetics, and psychosocial factors. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which route does the lecture recommend for breakthrough symptoms, and in whom is it a poor choice?","choices":["Inhalation, a poor choice in chronic lung disease or asthma","Oral, and it is a poor choice in people with malabsorption","Topical cream, and it is a poor choice in people with skin disease","Suppository, and it is a poor choice in people with constipation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls inhalation good for breakthrough symptoms and poor for people with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or asthma triggered by inhalants. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which treatment methods does the lecture list as part of an Ayurvedic treatment plan?","choices":["Diet, herbs, massage, meditation, yoga, and internal cleansing","Needles, cupping vessels, scraping tools, and spinal adjustment","Diluted remedies, succussion, and matched symptom prescribing","Sensors, monitors, real-time displays, and guided practice sessions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that after determining the balance of doshas, practitioners design an individualized plan using diet, herbs, massage, meditation, yoga, and internal cleansing, also called therapeutic elimination. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which technique uses a segmental table?","choices":["Sacro Occipital Technique, using cranial sacral procedures","Activator Method, using a handheld instrument","Flexion and distraction","Logan, which treats muscle and soft tissue with gentle pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs flexion and distraction with a segmental table. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does the lecture define the focus of mind-body medicine?","choices":["The use of naturally occurring substances to promote health or manage disease","The application of hands-on technique to the musculoskeletal system and soft tissues","The interactions between the brain, mind, body, and behavior and their influence on health","The manipulation of proposed energy fields believed to surround and permeate the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines mind-body medicine as focusing on interactions among the brain, mind, body, and behavior and how those interactions influence health. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"From which language and meaning is the term Ayurveda derived, according to the lecture?","choices":["Greek, from words meaning \"like\" and \"disease\"","Chinese, from separate words meaning \"vital force\" and \"energy pathway\"","Latin, from words meaning \"nature\" and \"healing power\"","Sanskrit, from ayur meaning life and veda meaning knowledge or science"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that Ayurveda comes from the Sanskrit words ayur, meaning life, and veda, meaning knowledge or science. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which supplement is described as a precursor to glutathione and used to treat acetaminophen toxicity?","choices":["Alpha lipoic acid","Methylsulfonylmethane","N-acetylcysteine","Bromelain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes N-acetylcysteine as a precursor to glutathione, an antioxidant and a mucolytic, used to treat or prevent acetaminophen overdose and toxicity. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What must an emotional support animal letter document to secure Fair Housing Act protections?","choices":["The need for the animal within mental health treatment","The animal's completed training hours","The animal's breed and weight for the landlord's records","The owner's diagnosis in full clinical detail"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the letter documents the individual's need for an emotional support animal as part of mental health treatment, and is required to be eligible for Fair Housing Act protections. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In what year, and for which group, was human-animal interaction first used in the United States?","choices":["1867, for people with disabilities in a German community","1944, for wounded and disabled veterans working with farm animals","1919, for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder","1860, for patients with chronic illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates the first United States use of human-animal interaction, companionship with dogs, to 1919 for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does the lecture give as the licensure requirement for massage therapists?","choices":["No formal requirement in most states","500 to 1,000 hours by state, plus a Federation licensing examination","2,700 hours and the national certification examinations","A bachelor's degree followed by national board parts one to four"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Continuing education is also required, and most states including Florida require licensure to practise. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which examination format does hippotherapy certification use?","choices":["A practical riding assessment","A multiple-choice examination","An oral board examination","A portfolio of documented cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states applicants must have passed a multiple-choice examination, in addition to the practice-hour requirements. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which statement reflects how Traditional Chinese Medicine approaches illness, according to the lecture?","choices":["It treats the disease in isolation from the person","It separates psyche from soma so each can be treated","It rejects illness that cannot yet be explained","It considers the person in association with the rest of nature and the social setting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes holistic patterns of illness and interrelated organ patterns. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about cannabis use in young adults and psychosis?","choices":["Studies show it prevents psychosis in high risk individuals","Studies suggest increased psychosis risk in high risk individuals","No association has been examined in young adults","The risk applies only to products high in cannabidiol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes studies suggest cannabis use in young adults increases the risk of developing psychosis in high risk individuals. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe Tai Chi?","choices":["A competitive martial sport organised around graded belts","A Chinese internal martial art performed slowly with deep breathing","A vigorous aerobic routine performed to music in a class","A seated meditation practice involving no physical movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as noncompetitive and self-paced, with each posture flowing into the next without pause. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Does the Americans with Disabilities Act require service animals to be professionally trained?","choices":["Yes, through an accredited service dog organisation","No, handlers may train the dog themselves","Yes, with annual recertification","Only for psychiatric service dogs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the Act does not require professional training and that people with disabilities may train the dog themselves without using a professional program. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
